A||N||11||strick8||88 V6 and 87 V6||||1||
Z||000000||strick8||11-10-2000||10:54 PM||||What is the difference between the 88 V6 and 87 V6? Can all the parts off an 87 engine be used on the 88?||||reg||1
Z||000001||1FST2M6||11-10-2000||11:05 PM||||yes they can be interchanged.. but the 86 cannot.. the 87s have splayed valves where the 86s don't||||reg||1
Z||000002||fiero56||11-10-2000||11:06 PM||||There is a thread from a few months back with all that information. I don't remember the answer.||||reg||1
Z||000003||fiero56||11-10-2000||11:07 PM||||I thought only aluminum heads had splayed valves?||||reg||1
Z||000004||FIEROCIOUS||11-11-2000||01:02 AM||||88-87 had splayed valves? Whatever! If you don't know don't answer!<P>no information is better than bad information!<P>87-88 2.8 V6's had a differant timeing chain/oil pan set up then the 85-86 V6's<P>87-88 are the same, they both have a one piece oil pan gasket, were as the 85-86 have a three piece set. 2.8 S-10 oil pan and timeing cover are the same as 85-86 Fiero's.<BR>87-88 oil pan and timeing cover are Fiero specific only.<P>||||reg||1
Z||000005||mrfiero||11-11-2000||01:07 AM||||Don't forget that the '88 V-6 is neutrally balanced.   That means that you must use the flywheel/flexplate that goes with the engine.<p>[This message has been edited by mrfiero (edited 11-11-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000006||88formula||11-11-2000||03:40 PM||||FIEROCIOUS and Mrfiero are right.<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000007||rockcrawl||11-11-2000||06:21 PM||||I must agree with the two previous posts. I just finished putting an 86 engine in my 87. I retained the 87 timing cover and oil pan because they are of a better design. otherwise the two engines are identical. The '87 also had improved exhaust manifolds, although both years were cracked. And yes, the 88's are internally ballanced and require a nuetrally ballanced flywheel. Not sure if the crank is the same.<P>------------------<BR>Jon Lagler<BR>'87 GT<BR>4.9 liter V8 Testarossa Spyder (under construction)||||reg||1
Z||000008||StuGood||11-11-2000||06:48 PM||||I've done considerable research on this very subject.  Pored over reams of drawings, GM internal memos, photographs, and various other sorts of documentation.  To best of my knowledge, the '88 V6 is one year newer than the '87 V6.  Ummm, and I dunno what parts are interchangeable, and what ain't.<P>Glad to be of assistance... <P>   ||||reg||12
Z||000009||fiero56||11-11-2000||09:22 PM||||'87 & '88 2.8 are different, other than just being balanced differently.<p>[This message has been edited by fiero56 (edited 11-12-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000010||strick8||11-14-2000||05:57 PM||||Ok? Let me ask this then, can I use a 87 crank in a 88 engine?||||reg||5
Z||000011||mrfiero||11-14-2000||06:20 PM||||Yes.....just make sure to use the flywheel/flexplate from the '87. ||||reg||1
